{"step_index":365,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-06T02:53:20Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-06T11:53:20+09:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-06T11:53:22+09:00\nShowing lines 1-137 of 137\n1: # Google Colab (T4 GPU) 遠隔第一原理MD & 表面破壊エネルギー計算規約\n2: \n3: Google Colaboratory (T4 GPU) 上で MDI (LAMMPS + Quantum ESPRESSO) 連成計算および表面破壊エネルギー評価を行う際は、本規約に厳格に従うこと。\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ## 1. Google Drive 自動同期・バックアップ規約\n8: * **パスの対応関係**:\n9:   * Windows 側: `G:\\マイドライブ\\MD_backup\\`\n10:   * Colab 側: `/content/drive/MyDrive/MD_backup/`\n11:   * 両者は完全同期ディレクトリであるため、計算プロセス中の自動バックアップ先は必ず `/content/drive/MyDrive/MD_backup/` を指定すること。\n12: * **バックアップループ**:\n13:   * `master_run_gpu.sh` 内で 5分間隔のバックグラウンドループ（`while true; do ... sleep 300; done &`）を実行し、以下の拡張子を同期すること：\n14:     `*.lammpstrj`, `*.xyz`, `*.log`, `*.pwo`, `*.pwi`, `nohup.out`\n15: * **結果ファイルの上書き防止と個別アーカイブ規約（厳格義務）**:\n16:   * 既存の計算結果（`dump.lammpstrj`, `espresso.pwo`, `md_animation.xyz`, `log.lammps`, `qe_engine.log` 等）を、後続の別計算（バルク計算、他の方位、異なるカットオフ等）によって直接上書きしたり、スクリプトの `rm -rf` で安易に一括消去することは**絶対禁止**。\n17:   * 各計算が完了した際、または次の新しい計算を同一環境で開始する前には、必ずモデル名・面方位・計算種別・日付を付与した独立ディレクトリ（例: `2H-SiC_10-10_slab_relaxed_YYYYMMDD/`）を Google Drive およびローカルに作成し、全成果物を完全退避・保護すること。\n18:   * 今回の成果物は `2H-SiC_10-10_slab_relaxed_20261020/`（Google Drive: `G:\\マイドライブ\\MD_backup\\2H-SiC_10-10_slab_relaxed_20261020\\`、ローカル: `results_2\n<truncated 8040 bytes>\n経過時間を ANSI カラー付きでリアルタイム表示する。生ログ表示は `--raw`。\n119: 2. **エディタ画面へのリアルタイム同期 (`live_espresso.pwo`)**:\n120:    - バックグラウンドでリモートログをローカルの `live_espresso.pwo` へ `tail -f` 転送し、ユーザーが IDE エディタでファイルを開いているだけで末尾にリアルタイム追記される環境を提供する。\n121: 3. **ワンショット進捗診断 (`check_progress.py`)**:\n122:    - リモートのプロセス稼働状況、GPUメモリ/使用率、最新の SCF 収束ステップをスナップショット判定するツールを常備する。\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 9. 指示書テキストファイル経由の 3 種推移グラフ自動生成・保存定例化システム\n127: * **標準指示書 (`RELAXATION_GRAPH_PROTOCOL.json`) の必須配備**:\n128:   ワークスペースルートおよび各計算ディレクトリに、3 種推移グラフの体裁（Word 最適化 8.0cm × 6.2cm, 600dpi, PNG/PDF/SVG, MS明朝 10.5pt, Times New Roman 9.5pt, 中抜き紫三角 `#8A2BE2`）を定義した指示書テキストファイルを常備すること。\n129: * **汎用自動生成エンジン (`auto_generate_progression_graphs.py`)**:\n130:   計算実行スクリプト（`run_colab_gpu.sh`）の終了部（`wait $LMP_PID` 直後）には、必ず以下を組み込むこと：\n131:   ```bash\n132:   python3 auto_generate_progression_graphs.py\n133:   ```\n134: * **自動保存先解決ルール**:\n135:   `auto_generate_progression_graphs.py` は、同ディレクトリの `master_run_gpu.sh` 内の `BACKUP_DIR`（Google Drive パス）を自動検出し、ローカル計算ディレクトリと Google Drive バックアップフォルダの両方へ即座に 3 種グラフ（①全エネルギー推移、③全原子作用力推移、④SCF補正値推移）を自動ダイレクト保存すること。手動操作や後付け生成に依存してはならない。\n136: \n137: ","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":366,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-06T02:53:21Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-06T11:53:21+09:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-06T11:53:22+09:00\nShowing lines 1-85 of 174\n1: # Surface Fracture Energy & 2H-SiC Slab Model Standards\n2: \n3: When assisting with model generation, theoretical explanation, or validation of surface fracture energy calculations for 2H-SiC, adhere strictly to these principles:\n4: \n5: ## 1. Surface Fracture Energy Definition\n6: - **Formula**:\n7:   $$\\gamma = \\frac{\\Delta E}{2A} = \\frac{E_{\\text{slab}}(N) - E_{\\text{bulk}}(N)}{2A}$$\n8: - **Equal Number of Atoms ($N$)**: The slab model ($E_{\\text{slab}}$) and the bulk reference model ($E_{\\text{bulk}}$) MUST have the exact same number of atoms and stoichiometry (\"same size cake\").\n9: - **Denominator $2A$**: Cleaving along 1 plane of cross-sectional area $A$ creates **two** surfaces (top and bottom), resulting in a total newly created surface area of $2A$.\n10: - **Physical Meaning of $\\Delta E$**: $\\Delta E$ represents the chemical bond breaking energy PLUS the surface structural relaxation energy (reconstruction / strain release of under-coordinated surface atoms). It is NOT the intrinsic energy of the surface atoms alone.\n11: \n12: ## 2. Material & Crystal Plane Selection\n13: - **Material: 2H-SiC (Wurtzite)**\n14:   - *Primary reason*: Smallest periodic stacking repeat along the c-axis (1 repeat = 2 layers), minimizing unit cell dimensions and DFT/MD computational cost.\n15:   - *Secondary reason*: Inomata & Inoue (1970) establishes 2H-SiC as a stable phase below 1400°C (including room temperature ~30°C).\n16: - **Crystal Plane: $\\{10\\bar{1}0\\}$ Prismatic Plane (m-plane)**\n17:   - *Non-polar Surface*: Stoichiometric 1:1 ratio of Si and C in every layer. Net dipole moment along $z$ is strictly zero (Tasker Type I), preventing artificial electrostatic divergence in the vacuum layer (unlike polar $(0001)$ basal planes).\n18:   - *Minimal Complexity*: Avoids complex tilted pyramidal planes (e.g., $\\{10\\bar{1}1\\}$ or $\\{11\\bar{2}2\\}$), having minimal unit cell area ($a \\times c$) compared to $\\{11\\bar{2}0\\}$ (which \n<truncated 4582 bytes>\ny_results.csv`.\n60: - **Zero-Manual Post-Processing**:\n61:   - Upon completion of any slab structural relaxation or bulk SCF calculation, the agent MUST automatically invoke:\n62:     ```bash\n63:     python3 modular_aimd_framework/parameters_variable/calc_surface_energy.py --auto \\\n64:       --slab-dir <SLAB_DIR> --bulk-dir <BULK_DIR>\n65:     ```\n66:   - This extracts $E_{\\text{slab}}$, $E_{\\text{bulk}}$, calculates $\\Delta E$ and $\\gamma$, updates both Markdown/CSV ledgers, and mirrors the results to Google Drive (`G:\\マイドライブ\\MD_backup\\`).\n67: \n68: ## 7. 300 K NVT Ab Initio Molecular Dynamics (AIMD) Standards\n69: When configuring finite-temperature AIMD for 2H-SiC slabs:\n70: 1. **Initial Structure**:\n71:    - MUST use the **0 K fully relaxed structure** (`slab_10-10_relaxed.data`) to prevent unphysical bond breaking and SCF divergence caused by unrelaxed forces ($F_{\\max} \\approx 2.55\\text{ eV/\\AA}$).\n72: 2. **Time Step (`timestep`)**:\n73:    - Set to `0.001 ps` ($1.0\\text{ fs}$). This safely resolves the fastest optical phonon vibration of 2H-SiC ($\\sim 33\\text{ fs}$, ratio $> 1/30$).\n74: 3. **Thermostat (`fix nvt`)**:\n75:    - Nosé-Hoover thermostat with damping time $T_{\\text{damp}} = 0.1\\text{ ps}$ ($100\\text{ fs} = 100 \\times \\Delta t$, standard LAMMPS ratio).\n76: 4. **Center-of-Mass Drift Prevention (`fix momentum`)**:\n77:    - For slab geometries with vacuum, MUST include `fix 3 all momentum 50 linear 1 1 1` to prevent rigid-body drift across PBC boundaries.\n78: 5. **Standard Simulation Horizon**:\n79:    - Default: $1,000\\text{ steps}$ ($1.0\\text{ ps}$).\n80:      - Steps 1–500 ($0.5\\text{ ps}$): Thermal equilibration (temperature stabilizes at 300 K).\n81:      - Steps 501–1,000 ($0.5\\text{ ps}$): Thermal trajectory sampling (MSD, average surface configuration).\n82:    - Execution time: $\\approx 5.5\\text{ hours}$ on Tesla T4 GPU (safe within single Colab session).\n83: \n84: ## 8. Vacuum Layer Thickness & Interaction Cutoff Threshold\n85: - **Benchmark Findings (2H-SiC $\\{10\\bar{1}0\\}$ Slab)**:","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":367,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-06T02:53:21Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-06T11:53:21+09:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-06T11:53:22+09:00\nShowing lines 1-11 of 11\n1: # 2H-SiC Supercell Construction Rules\n2: \n3: When assisting with the construction of 2H-SiC supercells or interpreting user requests regarding SiC layers, adhere to the following rules:\n4: \n5: 1. **Unit Cell Knowledge**: The basic 2H-SiC unit cell already contains **2 layers** of Si-C atom pairs (A and B stacking) along the c-axis.\n6: 2. **Expansion Factor Math**: Expanding the unit cell by a factor of `N` along the c-axis will result in `2 × N` layers total.\n7:    - For example, a `3x3x2` supercell yields **4 layers** along the c-axis.\n8:    - To achieve exactly 2 layers (the original thickness), the expansion factor must be `3x3x1`.\n9: 3. **User Clarification**: If a user requests a supercell dimensions like `3x3x2` while stating they want \"2 layers,\" kindly correct their misunderstanding before proceeding.\n10: 4. **Tooling**: Use Python and the `ase` (Atomic Simulation Environment) library (`ase.build.make_supercell`) to construct these structures automatically.\n11: 5. **Output Formats**: When generating supercells, output both `.cif` (to preserve lattice information) and `.xyz` (for immediate visualization in tools like Winmostar)."}
{"step_index":368,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-06T02:53:21Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-06T11:53:21+09:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-06T11:53:22+09:00\nShowing lines 1-19 of 19\n1: ---\n2: description: CIFファイルの閲覧とWinmostarの取り扱いに関するルール\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # CIF File Viewing & Winmostar\n6: \n7: この環境はLinuxベースで動作しているため、Windows専用のGUIアプリケーションである「Winmostar」をエディタ上で直接起動することはできません。\n8: \n9: ユーザーが「CIFファイルをWinmostarで開きたい」「エディタ上で3D構造を見たい」と要望した場合は、以下の手順のいずれかを提案・実行してください。\n10: \n11: 1. **Winmostarで開きたい場合（ローカルでの実行）:**\n12:    対象のCIFファイルをローカルのWindows PCへダウンロードし、手元のWinmostarで開くように案内する。\n13: \n14: 2. **エディタ上で3D構造を確認したい場合（推奨）:**\n15:    - エディタの拡張機能（`3Dmol` など）のインストール方法を案内する。\n16:    - または、`py3Dmol` を使用してCIFファイルをHTMLに変換するPythonスクリプト（`cif_viewer.py`など）を提供・実行し、生成されたHTMLファイルをエディタ上で開くように案内する。\n17: \n18: **禁止事項:**\n19: - Linux環境内で `winmostar` コマンドを探したり、インストールを試みること。"}
{"step_index":369,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-06T02:53:21Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-06T11:53:21+09:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-06T11:53:22+09:00\nShowing lines 1-18 of 18\n1: # Peer Review & Academic Coaching Rules for SiC Fracture Research\n2: \n3: When interacting with the user regarding their research on SiC surface fracture energy and slab models (especially in preparation for posters, presentations, and defense), strictly follow these rules:\n4: \n5: ## 1. Coaching Style (Peer Reviewer Persona)\n6: - **Do Not Give Away Answers Immediately**: Follow the professor's guidance from seminars: do not overwhelm the user with ready-made answers. Encourage the user to explain and reason in their own words.\n7: - **Peer Reviewer Perspective**: Act as a rigorous, knowledgeable peer reviewer / committee member specializing in density functional theory (DFT), molecular dynamics (MD), crystallography, and fracture mechanics.\n8: - **Literature-Grounded Questions**: Frame questions and counter-arguments based on established academic literature (e.g., Rauls et al. 1999, Inomata & Inoue 1970, King & Antonelli 2007) and physical principles.\n9: - **Socratic Method**: Point out physical, geometric, or terminological inconsistencies in the user's answers, and prompt them with targeted follow-up questions to guide them to the correct scientific consensus.\n10: \n11: ## 2. Command Execution & Scripting Constraints\n12: - **NO Autonomous Simulation Runs**: NEVER start expensive computational calculations (e.g., executing `pw.x`, `lmp`, or launching Colab workflows) without explicit user confirmation. Always stop at model generation, script preparation, and geometric/stoichiometric verification.\n13: - **Avoid `python3 -c \"...\"` One-Liners**: Do NOT execute multiline or complex Python code via inline `python3 -c` shell commands, as they frequently cause syntax/escaping errors.\n14: - **Write Standalone Scripts**: Always write verification and analysis tools as clean Python files (e.g., `check_polarity.py`, `create_10-10_slab_3x3x2.py`) saved in the workspace, and execute them as `python3 <script_name>.py`.\n15: \n16: ## 3. Visualization and Tooling\n17: - Support viewing structures in Winmostar (via `view_slab_winmostar.py` or `.bashrc` alias `wm`).\n18: - Output all atomic models in `.cif` (preserving periodic cell vectors and boundary conditions), `.xyz` (for quick visualizers), and `.data` (for LAMMPS)."}
